.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
This function is only available on Cygwin.
On other platforms, the function returns NULL with \f[CR]errno\f[R] set
to \f[CR]ENOSYS\f[R].
Use \f[CR]bc_connect(3)\f[R] for portable Unix domain socket
connections.
.IP \(bu 2
The Named Pipe transport uses the same protocol as the Unix socket
transport.
After connection, all API functions (\f[CR]bc_auth\f[R],
\f[CR]bc_eval\f[R], etc.)
work identically regardless of transport.
.IP \(bu 2
The server must have been started with \f[CR]\-\-named\-pipe <name>\f[R]
to accept Named Pipe connections.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows Named Pipes have a maximum instance count configured on the
server side.
If all instances are busy, the connection will fail with
\f[CR]ECONNREFUSED\f[R].
Retry after a short delay if needed.
.IP \(bu 2
The pipe path uses forward slashes on Cygwin (\f[CR]//./pipe/name\f[R])
rather than backslashes (\f[CR]\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsname\f[R]).
Both forms are accepted.
.IP \(bu 2
Named Pipe security is governed by a DACL set by the server at pipe
creation time.
By default, only the same user can connect.
See \f[CR]bash\-server\-security(7)\f[R] for details.
